// @check
// @error no `tag` on `T` — `T` is a type parameter with no bound, and a parameter's members come from its bound. Declare one: `<T: { tag: … }>`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `b` is inferred as `{tag: () => string}` — remove it to lean on inference
// THE SAME FLOOR REACHED BY THE OTHER ROUTE (D1221). A member CALL on an unbounded
// `<T>` tries the bounded-member route, then UFCS dispatch by name, and lands on the
// member floor only when both come up empty — which needs the one line nobody would
// write: NO free `self`-function called `tag` anywhere in the program. Add
// `function tag(self: Circle): string` for a type this program never instantiates
// `g` at and the same program compiles and prints `fld`, because the selection is by
// NAME inside a generic body and the pin then sees a receiver carrying the FIELD.
//
// So every fixture in the field-dispatch family carries a `self`-function — the
// family is ABOUT the precedence between the two — and this shape is the one none of
// them could see.
type Boxed = { tag: () => string }

function g<T>(x: T): string {
  x.tag()
}

const b: Boxed = { tag: () => "fld" }
print(g(b))
